Z||000004||rogergarrison||08-13-2001||12:05 PM||||u have to be careful of you color selections to make sure that a smooth blend will work. Laquer works by far better in blends, so youll have to make sure that the colors you want are available. Laquer for most part is not available anymore except for existing supplies. Experiment around, some colors will absolutely not blend together. I havent done any using basecoat colors so again you have to experiment a little. I would think just by being high pigment colors that they wouldnt make blended transitions very well. to blend effectively too you have to start with litest color first, then darker color has to be thinned much more than normal and you have to use higher air pressure. an HVLP type gun wouldnt work, it would have to be a syphon feed. you would have to be VERY careful not to touch any part until after it has at least 1 coat of clear. any touch will smear the dark color at that spot and you would have to start all over again. you also have to have good gun control, able to keep your passes straight to avoid a wavey blend line. good luck. remember experiment on some old panels first to try getting the effect you want.<P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=34556781"> ||||reg||1
